Optimize mobilnet_v2 on CPU by 50-100x by optimizing MathBackendCPU (#1210)

Optimize mobilenet_v2 on CPU by 50x-100x by optimizing MathBackendCPU.

This optimization was motivated by trying to speedup mobilenet_v2 on the cpu backend using the following benchmark:

```html
<script src="tf-core.js"></script>
<script src="tf-converter.js"></script>
<script>
  let model;

  function load() {
    const MODEL_URL = './tensorflowjs_model.pb';
    const WEIGHTS_URL = './weights_manifest.json';
    tf.loadFrozenModel(MODEL_URL, WEIGHTS_URL).then(m => {
      model = m;
    });
  }

  function predict() {
    const test = tf.zeros([1, 224, 224, 3]);
    // Warmup.
    model.execute(test).dataSync();

    const start = performance.now();
    model.execute(test).dataSync();
    const elapsed = performance.now() - start;
    console.log('Took', elapsed, 'ms');
  }

  tf.setBackend('cpu');
  load();
</script>
<button onclick="predict();">Predict</button>
```
#### Results (on a desktop Linux)
- Before
  - Firefox: 79438 ms
  - Chrome: 36554 ms
- After
  - Firefox: 650 ms 
  - Chrome: 812 ms

#### Details:

- optimize `conv2d()` and `depthwiseConv2d()` by avoiding calls to `tensor.get(....)` and using flat indexing into a typed array.
- optimize `clip` by using ternary ops instead of `Math.min/max`
- optimize broadcasting in `batchNormalization` by doing manual wrapping instead of `%` for the index.
- optimize all binary ops by testing if broadcasting happens along the inner most axis, at which point, the indexing logic gets significantly simpler.

There is still a lot of low-hanging fruit in MathBackendCPU - any kernel that uses `tensor.get()`. External contributions are welcome.

Also fix a small bug where `yarn test --backend cpu` was still running webgl tests, because the `HAS_WEBGL` env. variable wasn't set to `false`.

PERF

